# Environment for the Jetson. Copy to ~/aeroshield/.env on the drone.
#
#   scp jetson/.env.example jetson:~/aeroshield/.env
#   ssh jetson 'nano ~/aeroshield/.env'
#
# `make run` and `make stream` both do `set -a; . ~/aeroshield/.env; set +a`
# before invoking live_detect.py, which is why the key lives here and not in the
# Makefile: it is a credential, and the Makefile is committed.
#
# The real .env is gitignored (`.gitignore` covers `.env` everywhere but
# `.env.example`), so nothing here is secret. Never put a working key in this
# template.

# Ingest credential the drone presents as X-API-Key on POST /api/detections.
# Scope needed: drone:ingest. Mint it on the machine running the backend:
#
#   python backend/scripts/create_api_key.py --name drone-01 --preset drone
#
# The value is printed once and cannot be recovered - if it is lost, mint another.
#
# Unset, the drone still runs: it logs every detection locally and spools them to
# disk, and the backend rejects each POST with 401. That looks like a silent
# dashboard rather than an auth error, so check this first when nothing arrives.
AEROSHIELD_API_KEY=

# The simulated origin and flight geometry are NOT set here - they are arguments
# the Makefile passes (`--sim-origin`, `--sim-leg-length`, `--sim-lane-spacing`,
# `--sim-lanes`), so they can be changed per run without editing a file on the
# drone. See SIM_FLAGS in the top-level Makefile.
